AZ-104: Prerequisites for Azure Administrators

Platform: Microsoft
Course: AZ-104: Prerequisites for Azure Administrators
Level: Beginner
Role Path: Azure Administrator
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn the foundational cloud and Azure concepts required for the Microsoft AZ-104 Azure Administrator certification path. This learning path introduces core Azure services, identity concepts, networking basics, compute resources, storage, and governance tools used by Azure administrators in real-world cloud environments.

Step 1: Introduction to Azure Administration

The learning path introduced the responsibilities of an Azure Administrator and the core services used to manage cloud infrastructure within Microsoft Azure.

Main responsibilities of an Azure Administrator include:

Managing cloud resources
Configuring virtual networks
Deploying virtual machines
Managing storage solutions
Monitoring cloud infrastructure
Implementing identity and access management
Maintaining security and governance

The course also explained how Azure administrators support organizations by maintaining scalable and reliable cloud environments.

Key takeaway:
Azure administrators are responsible for deploying, managing, and monitoring cloud infrastructure services in Azure.

Step 2: Understanding Cloud Computing Concepts

The module reviewed essential cloud computing principles.

Cloud service models:

Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)
Platform as a Service (PaaS)
Software as a Service (SaaS)

Cloud deployment models:

Public cloud
Private cloud
Hybrid cloud

Benefits of cloud computing:

Scalability
High availability
Elasticity
Cost efficiency
Disaster recovery
Global accessibility

Key takeaway:
Understanding cloud concepts is critical before managing Azure infrastructure.

Step 3: Azure Architecture and Core Services

The course introduced Azure’s global infrastructure and resource organization hierarchy.

Core Azure components:

Regions
Availability Zones
Resource Groups
Subscriptions
Management Groups

Important services covered:

Azure Virtual Machines
Azure Virtual Network
Azure Storage Accounts
Azure Container Registry
Azure Container Instances
Microsoft Entra ID

The module explained how resources are logically grouped and managed within Azure environments.

Key takeaway:
Azure resources are organized using subscriptions and resource groups to simplify administration and governance.

Step 4: Microsoft Entra ID and Identity Management

The learning path introduced identity and access management using Microsoft Entra ID.

Topics covered:

Authentication vs authorization
Users and groups
Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
Identity security principles

RBAC allows administrators to assign permissions based on job responsibilities while following the principle of least privilege.

Key takeaway:
Identity and access management is a core responsibility of Azure administrators.

Step 5: Azure Compute Resources

The course explored compute services available in Azure.

Compute resources covered:

Azure Virtual Machines
Virtual Machine Scale Sets
Containers
Azure Container Instances

Concepts learned:

VM sizing
Operating system images
Resource allocation
Scalability
Availability

The module also introduced basic container concepts and cloud-based application deployment.

Key takeaway:
Azure provides flexible compute solutions for both traditional workloads and containerized applications.

Step 6: Azure Networking Basics

The networking section introduced the foundational components used to connect Azure resources securely.

Topics covered:

Azure Virtual Networks (VNets)
Subnets
IP addressing
Network Security Groups (NSGs)
Public and private endpoints

The course explained how Azure networking isolates resources while enabling secure communication between systems.

Key takeaway:
Networking is essential for secure and reliable cloud infrastructure management.

Step 7: Azure Storage Services

The learning path covered Azure storage solutions used to store cloud data.

Storage services introduced:

Blob Storage
File Storage
Queue Storage
Table Storage

Important concepts:

Redundancy options
Storage tiers
Secure access
Data availability

The course emphasized the importance of selecting appropriate storage solutions based on workload requirements.

Key takeaway:
Azure offers multiple scalable storage services for different business and application needs.

Step 8: Azure Governance and Management Tools

The module introduced governance and monitoring tools available in Azure.

Tools and concepts covered:

Azure Policy
Resource Locks
Tags
Azure Monitor
Cost Management
Azure CLI

The course also introduced automation and command-line management using Azure CLI.

Key takeaway:
Governance tools help organizations maintain compliance, security, and cost control across cloud environments.

Skills Learned
Understanding cloud computing models
Azure architecture fundamentals
Identity and access management basics
Azure networking fundamentals
Virtual machine deployment concepts
Storage management basics
Governance and monitoring principles
Introduction to Azure administration tools
Conclusion

The AZ-104 Prerequisites for Azure Administrators learning path provided a strong foundation in cloud computing and Microsoft Azure services.

The course introduced the essential concepts needed before progressing deeper into Azure administration topics such as identity management, networking, compute resources, storage, and monitoring.

Completing this learning path builds the foundational knowledge required for the AZ-104 certification and prepares learners for real-world Azure administration responsibilities in cloud and cybersecurity environments.
